WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:06.480
There are many theoretical reasons for believing
that there is much more to our universe than

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:08.970
the part that we can see.

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:10.830
And this idea is called the "multiverse."

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:17.230
It's the idea that there are other pocket
universes far beyond what we can actually

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:22.880
see within our observable horizon and that
those universes could have very different

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:24.410
properties to our own.

00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:26.510
So this is almost like science fiction.

00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:29.890
When I first heard about this idea, I thought,
"It's not testable."

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:36.890
I'm an experimental scientist and if something
can't be tested, for me it remains theoretical

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:37.890
or speculation.

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:44.760
So when I met Matt Johnson and he suggested
to me that there was a way to test this idea,

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:47.079
I became very excited.

00:00:47.079 --> 00:00:52.039
Because we have to be very lucky to be able
to test this idea, but if it can be tested

00:00:52.039 --> 00:00:56.520
— it's discovering another universe is an
amazing thing.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:00.840
And the way that this could be tested is that
very early on, when these bubble universes

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:03.960
are very close together, they can collide.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:11.020
And that collision can cause a perturbation
or a bruise in what we see in the very earliest

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:15.729
light in our universe: the cosmic microwave
background.

00:01:15.729 --> 00:01:21.700
So we've developed these algorithms to try
to extract such a signature from this data.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:28.079
While we were doing this observational program,
we realized that the predictions for what

00:01:28.079 --> 00:01:33.490
the signal should look like should also be
revised because when these collisions happen,

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:35.560
it's a very violent event.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:39.969
It could actually cause the destruction of
one of the bubble universes.

00:01:39.969 --> 00:01:46.829
The mathematical techniques that we had to
try to predict that signature were not enough.

00:01:46.829 --> 00:01:53.649
We learnt that we have to put Einstein's equations
for general relativity on a computer and do

00:01:53.649 --> 00:01:55.999
this problem numerically.

00:01:55.999 --> 00:01:56.999
So that's what we did.

00:01:56.999 --> 00:01:59.809
We actually simulated whole universes in the
computer.

00:01:59.809 --> 00:02:01.340
We collided them.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:04.349
And we were able to extract the observational
signature.

00:02:04.349 --> 00:02:08.490
This now allows us to carry on this observational
program for looking for this signature, and

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:09.590
I'm very excited about that.

